In article <22218@cfctech.cfc.com> kevin@cfctech.cfc.com (Kevin Darcy) writes:
>As far as reusability, I have to ask what games you are referring to? In D&D, 
>every time you cast a memorized spell, the "energy of the casting wipes it 
>from your memory" or some similar verbiage. It then has to be recouped in a
>rested, non-combat state. Other gaming systems I've been exposed to do things 
>similarly - the number of spells available in a given period of time is 
>quite severely limited. Why is this?

Been a long time since I looked at AD&D; I'm thinking primarily of
other computer RPG's I've played recently.  I don't have problems with
having spells 'drain' you sufficiently that you have to recover before
using them again -- but in NH it appears that they disappear forever
after N uses.  I can see the problem with allowing infinite casting of
spells.  However, there's the flip side, as well.  The magic-users are
the ONE class of character in NH whose 'primary weapons' (spells and
wands) intrinsically lose power with use.   A wizard is losing defensive
and offensive capability as he moves through the dungeon, until he's
lucky enough to find something else useable.

>Of course, maybe you have some other limitations on spell-casting in mind,
>such as limited "energy-points", or making the player hungry, both of which 
>are implemented in NetHack. 

Yeah, something like that.  'Magic points' which it costs to use a bit
of magic and which are replaced by time (or prayer?).  Or maybe, if
casting spells is considered to be physically wearing, costing 1d4 or
1d6 HP each time a spell is used.  Or, perhaps, a probability that a
spell will fizzle, with a fizzle 'decreasing your memory' of the spell
and so making it more likely to fizzle again.  (On grounds that if you
mis-speak it, it's helping you remember a wrong set of words.)

Nevermind.  Despite my occasional moan, I like the game.
